cff-version: 1.2.0
message: 'If you use lexsync, please cite the software. A manuscript describing it is in preparation.'
title: 'lexsync: Lexical optimisation and hardware-timed experiment generation'
abstract: >-
  lexsync is a cross-platform, dual-language (R and Python) toolkit that unifies
  many-language lexical-corpus access, parallel multidimensional stimulus
  matching, counterbalancing, item resampling, deterministic pseudoword
  generation and the automated generation of hardware-timed PsychoPy,
  OpenSesame and jsPsych experiment scripts for EEG/ERP and browser-based
  research. The MIT licence covers the source code; the bundled example lexica
  are derived from wordfreq (Speer, 2022) and are distributed under
  CC BY-SA 4.0, with the terms and the attribution they require in LICENSE-DATA
  and corpora/ATTRIBUTION.md.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Bernabeu
    given-names: Pablo
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1083-2460'
version: 0.1.1
# The DOI CRAN assigned to the R package on publication. It identifies the
# package across all its versions, so the version above pins the release.
doi: 10.32614/CRAN.package.lexsync
# CRAN distributes the built package, which is what CFF's repository-artifact
# field is for. The sources are on GitHub, in repository-code below.
repository-artifact: 'https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=lexsync'
# The date CRAN published the latest release. Still 0.1.0's until CRAN publishes
# 0.1.1, when the date moves with it.
date-released: '2026-09-22'
# Both DOIs, since doi holds one. Zenodo's concept DOI resolves to the latest
# archived GitHub release, which carries the Python package as well as the R one.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.32614/CRAN.package.lexsync
    description: The R package on CRAN, across all its versions.
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22906962
    description: Every GitHub release of the repository, archived on Zenodo.
# Two licences, because the release ships both: MIT for the code, CC BY-SA 4.0 for
# the wordfreq-derived example lexica inside each package.
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-SA-4.0
repository-code: 'https://github.com/pablobernabeu/lexsync'
keywords:
  - psycholinguistics
  - lexical statistics
  - stimulus matching
  - counterbalancing
  - pseudoword generation
  - EEG
  - ERP
  - PsychoPy
  - OpenSesame
  - jsPsych
  - FAIR
# No preferred-citation until the manuscript describing lexsync is accepted.
# GitHub's 'Cite this repository' button renders preferred-citation in place of
# the software record, so an entry for the unpublished manuscript sent readers to
# a work they could not read, and left out the DOI above.
